
  Wins: nothing expensive gets frozen — there's no format to regret. Arbitrary logic (conditional fields, "either email
  or phone") is just an [38;5;153mif[39m, where a schema needs new machinery. Least speculative option given you can't name the future
  forms.
  Costs: the repetitive part — loop the fields, collect messages, keep the error shape consistent — gets rewritten per
  form, which is a chunk of what you asked to eliminate. Reuse is real but shallower.

  [1mC. Native HTML5 constraint validation as the engine[0m

  Put the constraints in the markup ([38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminlength="8"[39m, [38;5;153mtype="email"[39m, [38;5;153mpattern="..."[39m), add [38;5;153mnovalidate[39m to suppress
  the browser's own popups, and the shared module is a thin renderer: on submit, walk the controls, read each one's
  [38;5;153mvalidationMessage[39m, display them consistently.

  Wins: by far the least code, and it applies to every form automatically with no schema and no registration.
  Accessibility and i18n of the default messages come free from the browser.
  Costs: cross-field rules have no native expression — password-confirm needs a [38;5;153msetCustomValidity[39m escape hatch bolted
  on, and once you need two of those the "no code" advantage is gone. Validation logic lives in HTML, so it can't be
  unit-tested as pure functions; you'd need a DOM in the test environment. Default messages are browser- and
  locale-dependent unless you override each one.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m You told me this needs to work across the app with forms you can't name yet, and A is the only
  one where adding form #2 doesn't mean rewriting the plumbing. B is the more honest YAGNI answer and I'd pick it if
  you'd said "one more form"; you didn't. C is genuinely tempting for how little code it is, but "logic in markup,
  untestable in Node, no cross-field rules" is a bad trade for a layer meant to be the app-wide standard.
